---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import JournalNav from '../../components/JournalNav.astro';

const lang = 'en';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Publication Ethics | Ayamatma"
  description="Publication ethics policy for Ayamatma: Journal of Vedantic Studies."
  lang={lang}
>
  <section class="section stack">
    <p class="eyebrow">Ethics</p>
    <h1 class="section-title">Publication Ethics</h1>
    <p class="muted">
      The journal requires source honesty, attribution, correction, and genre transparency.
    </p>
    <JournalNav />
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Basic commitments</h2>
      <p>
        Ayamatma will not knowingly publish plagiarism, fabricated citations, invented quotations,
        undisclosed recycled publication, or articles that misrepresent devotional assertion as scholarly
        demonstration.
      </p>

      <h2>Sources and translations</h2>
      <p>
        Authors working with Sanskrit or other source texts must identify the text, edition or source used
        where possible, and distinguish literal translation from interpretive rendering. When a translation
        is original, that should be stated. When a published translation is used, it must be cited.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections and retractions</h2>
      <p>
        Significant errors will be corrected with visible notices. If an article contains serious defects
        that invalidate its claims, the journal may publish an expression of concern or retract the article.
        Retractions will preserve the bibliographic record while making the reason clear.
      </p>

      <h2>Conflicts of interest</h2>
      <p>
        Authors, reviewers, and editors should disclose personal, institutional, financial, or ideological
        conflicts that could reasonably affect judgment. Disclosure does not automatically disqualify a work;
        concealment is the problem.
      </p>

      <h2>Use of AI tools</h2>
      <p>
        AI tools may assist with drafting, editing, translation checking, or source organization, but they
        cannot be treated as authorities. Authors remain responsible for every claim, citation, translation,
        and quotation.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
